  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>On the two Lérins islands, Sainte-Marguerite and Saint-Honorat, the Cannes tourist office lists beaches and coves "open to dogs", provided they are kept on a leash. Access is only by ferry from the port of Cannes.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The city of Cannes asks visitors to keep their dog on a leash on both islands, not to disturb the animals and to stay on the marked paths. As for the boats: Trans Côte d'Azur states that dogs are welcome on board and on Sainte-Marguerite island, on a leash, and the official Planaria listing (Saint-Honorat) specifies that "dogs are accepted provided they are kept on a leash". No rule was found for the other operator on the Sainte-Marguerite line, Horizon: check before booking. Smoking is banned on both islands, and waste must be taken back to the mainland.</p>
      <p>Sainte-Marguerite, managed by the Office National des Forêts, is the larger island (3.2 km long). The Cannes tourist office's 2026 press kit reminds visitors that bicycles are banned there, that dogs must be kept on a leash and that there are no bins on the island. The list of beaches and coves open to dogs published by the tourist office notably mentions the Pierres Hautes beach, the Laoute beach, the Sainte-Anne beach and the Dragon, Vengeur and Batterie coves. A perimeter path lets you walk round it in about 2h30, with many picnic tables (Wikipedia).</p>
      <p>Saint-Honorat, 1.5 km long with 3 km of coastline, belongs to the community of Cistercian monks of Lérins Abbey, who grow vines there. Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur Tourisme states "animals accepted on a leash only" and the tourist office lists ten beaches and coves open to dogs there (Tonnelle cove, Port beach, Abbey beach, Fort beach, among others). The abbey has removed all the bins on the island: waste goes back to Cannes. The walk round the island takes about 50 minutes.</p>
      <p>Ferries to Saint-Honorat leave from quai Saint-Pierre and those to Sainte-Marguerite from quai Laubeuf, in the old port of Cannes. In 2026, the adult return crossing costs €19.50 with Planaria and €18.50 to €19 to Sainte-Marguerite; no price is published for the dog.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: Sainte-Marguerite is larger, ideal for a proper forest walk before the swim; Saint-Honorat, home to a monastery, is smaller and quieter. Bring water and bags, there are no bins.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The port of Cannes, the departure point for the ferries, is full of terraces for a break before or after the crossing.</p>
    </div>

